      {
        "id": "E3",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "In Part 1 , you built a task agent on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore , a platform to build, connect, and optimize agents at scale, with any framework or model.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/govern-ai-agent-access-with-okta-cross-app-access-and-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-part-2/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-06T17:09:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-08T16:39:47.587Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E4",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The agent runs on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore runtime, a capability of Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, and acts for signed-in users through Okta Cross App Access (XAA).",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/govern-ai-agent-access-with-okta-cross-app-access-and-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-part-2/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-06T17:09:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-08T16:39:47.587Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E5",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Okta decided which agent might act for which user, and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity, a capability of Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, redeemed Okta’s grant inside the agent.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/govern-ai-agent-access-with-okta-cross-app-access-and-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-part-2/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-06T17:09:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-08T16:39:47.587Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E6",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway, a capability of Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, becomes the single front door and redeems Okta’s grant for each target.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/govern-ai-agent-access-with-okta-cross-app-access-and-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-part-2/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-06T17:09:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-08T16:39:47.587Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E7",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Policy in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore evaluates Cedar policies that bind each tool to the agent and the scope Okta delegated.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/govern-ai-agent-access-with-okta-cross-app-access-and-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-part-2/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-06T17:09:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-08T16:39:47.587Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E8",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "A second agent on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) joins under its own Okta identity, to show that the front door doesn’t care where an agent runs.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/govern-ai-agent-access-with-okta-cross-app-access-and-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-part-2/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-06T17:09:23.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-08T16:39:47.587Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E9",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "And Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Observability, a capability of Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, with Amazon CloudWatch gives you one place to read every decision, including Okta’s.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/govern-ai-agent-access-with-okta-cross-app-access-and-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-part-2/",
        "source": "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-06T17:09:23.000Z",
